Let me implement `forbidden_scan()` in the audit script:
```python
FORBIDDEN = ["sorry", "admit", "axiom", "unsafe", "native_decide", "proof_wanted"]

def strip_comments(text: str) -> str:
    # remove nested block comments /- ... -/ (including /-! and /-- doc comments), and -- line comments
    out = []
    i = 0
    depth = 0
    n = len(text)
    while i < n:
        if depth == 0 and text.startswith("--", i):
            j = text.find("\n", i)
            i = n if j == -1 else j
            continue
        if text.startswith("/-", i):
            depth += 1
            i += 2
            continue
        if depth > 0 and text.startswith("-/", i):
            depth -= 1
            i += 2
            continue
        if depth == 0:
            out.append(text[i])
        i += 1
    return "".join(out)
```
Then token search with regex `\b(sorry|admit|axiom|unsafe|native_decide|proof_wanted)\b`. Note `unsafe` may appear as `unsafe def`; `axiom` as `axiom foo`; also `Classical.choice` etc. are fine. Careful: the word "axiom" might appear in identifiers? No. But "unsafe" could appear in docstrings — stripped ✓.

